    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "In the past, people explored the oceans to find new trade routes. Today, we explore to understand the ocean itself."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "New technology is helping scientists reach deeper into the sea. Better "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "submarines",
            "ref": "v-submarine"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", robots, and cameras allow us to go where humans couldn’t go before. A new Chinese "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "vessel",
            "zh": "轮船"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " named Mengxiang, with the world’s deepest "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "drilling",
            "zh": "钻探"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " ability, can now reach 11,000 metres below the surface. It will help us learn more about the ocean and protect it."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Surprisingly, we know less about the deep ocean than about space. Only three people have reached the deepest point of the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Mariana Trench",
            "zh": "马里亚纳海沟"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", while twelve have walked on the moon. Although the ocean covers 71% of Earth, 95% of it "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "remains",
            "ref": "v-remain"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " unexplored."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Why is the deep sea so hard to study? The "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "pressure",
            "ref": "v-pressure"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " is "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "extremely",
            "ref": "v-extremely"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " high—like having 50 "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "jumbo jets",
            "zh": "大型喷气式飞机"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " on top of you. It is also completely dark, making it difficult to see anything."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Still, exploring the ocean is important for everyone. It affects our air, food, weather, and climate. Deep waters contain fish, oil, gas, and "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "rare",
            "ref": "v-rare"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "minerals",
            "ref": "v-mineral"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". They may even provide future medicines."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Understanding the ocean helps us protect our planet and discover new resources."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      }
    ]
  },
